build/
build-*/
*.o
*.a
*.so
.cache/
compile_commands.json

# Python bytecode. The seed generator is a script and is not part of the
# build, but running it leaves a __pycache__ behind, and it appeared the
# first time anything imported it rather than the first time it was run --
# which is why this is here and not next to the generator.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Where a fuzz run writes the corpus it grows.
#
# libFuzzer adds an entry for every input it reaches, named by the SHA-1 of its
# contents, and a run of half a minute across six harnesses produces about two
# thousand of them. That is a name rather than a decision: nothing chose those
# inputs, they are what one machine reached in one run, and the next run will
# reach others. Committing them is how a repository comes to contain two
# thousand files nobody chose.
#
# The pattern is a **directory**, not a filename shape, and that is the whole
# point of it. The seeds the harnesses read live in `fuzz/seeds` and every one
# of them is hand-written with a name that says what it is for -- `pe_dll.bin`,
# `seccomp_two_rules.pol`, `rsp_g.gdb` -- so they belong in the repository, and
# a corpus entry does not. The two are told apart by *where they are*, not by
# what they are called, because the earlier attempt told them apart by name and
# could not: a corpus entry is forty hex digits, so any rule loose enough to
# catch one also catches `pe_dll.bin`, and any rule tight enough to spare that
# is forty character classes on a single line, which is unreadable and gets
# miscounted -- the first version of it had forty-four of them and silently
# matched nothing while a shorter rule beneath it made the file look handled.
# A rule that fails open and is believed to be working is worse than no rule.
#
# So the harnesses are pointed at `fuzz/seeds` to *read* and at `fuzz/corpus`
# to *write*, and the run command in docs/RUNTIME.md says so.
fuzz/corpus/
# Local-only. This tree contains development aids that are not part of the
# project: reference implementations used as oracles during development,
# scratch targets, and the notes that go with them. They are excluded by
# directory rather than by file so that adding one is not a change to this
# list, and the exclusion is total so that a reference implementation can
# never become something the build or the test suite depends on. See
# docs/RUNTIME.md, milestone M5.
tests/local/
tools/local/
